might start thinking about how to find

277
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:55,480
the areas under other graphs.

278
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:56,960
I mean, we were lucky in the circle

279
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:58,640
problem that the relevant area turned

280
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,520
out to be a triangle, but imagine

281
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:02,560
instead something like a parabola, the

282
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:04,840
graph of x squared.

283
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:07,120
What's the area underneath that curve,

284
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:10,280
say between the values of x = 0 and x =

285
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:12,080
3?

286
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:15,320
Well, it's hard to think about, right?

287
00:10:15,320 --> 00:10:16,840
And let me reframe that question in a

288
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:18,480
slightly different way.

289
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:20,360
We'll fix that left end point in place

290
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,560
at zero, and let the right end point

291
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:25,080
vary.

292
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:29,800
Are you able to find a function A of X

293
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:31,520
that gives you the area under this

294
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:35,520
parabola between zero and X?

295
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:38,000
A function A of X like this is called an

296
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,360
integral of X squared.

297
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:42,080
Calculus holds within it the tools to

298
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:43,839
figure out what an integral like this

299
00:10:43,839 --> 00:10:46,280
is, but right now, it's just a mystery

300
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:48,280
function to us. We know it gives the

301
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:50,240
area under the graph of X squared

302
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:52,120
between some fixed left point and some

303
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:54,000
variable right point, but we don't know

304
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:55,560
what it is.

305
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:57,480
And again, the reason we care about this

306
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:59,560
kind of question is not just for the

307
00:10:59,560 --> 00:11:02,320
sake of asking hard geometry questions,

308
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:04,400
it's because many practical problems

309
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,240
that can be approximated by adding up a

310
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:08,720
large number of small things can be

311
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:11,040
reframed as a question about an area

312
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:13,480
under a certain graph.

313
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,080
And I'll tell you right now that finding

314
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:17,680
this area, this integral function, is

315
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:19,280
genuinely hard.

316
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:21,240
And whenever you come across a genuinely

317
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:23,800
hard question in math, a good policy is

318
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,880
to not try too hard to get at the answer

319
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:28,000
directly, since usually you just end up

320
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,960
banging your head against a wall.

321
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:32,200
Instead, play around with the idea with

322
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:34,600
no particular goal in mind. Spend some

323
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:36,440
time building up familiarity with the

324
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:38,400
interplay between the function defining

325
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:41,000
the graph, in this case x squared, and

326
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,160
the function giving the area.

327
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:46,760
In that playful spirit, if you're lucky,

328
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:48,800
here's something that you might notice.

329
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:50,880
When you slightly increase x by some

330
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:53,120
tiny nudge, dx,

331
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:55,280
look at the resulting change in area,

332
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:57,080
represented with this sliver that I'm

333
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:59,800
going to call dA, for a tiny difference

334
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:01,440
in area.

335
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:03,080
That sliver can be pretty well

336
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:05,400
approximated with a rectangle, one whose

337
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:08,080
height is x squared, and whose width is

338
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:09,600
dx.

339
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:11,280
And the smaller the size of that nudge,

340
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:14,200
dx, the more that sliver actually looks

341
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,680
like a rectangle.

342
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:18,480
Now, this gives us an interesting way to

343
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:20,760
think about how A of x is related to x

344
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:21,920
squared.

345
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:24,280
A change to the output of A, this little

346
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:27,760
dA, is about equal to x squared, where x

347
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:30,240
is whatever input you started at, times

348
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:32,760
dx, the little nudge to the input that

349
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:34,880
caused A to change.

350
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:38,120
Or rearranged, dA divided by dx, the

351
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,920
ratio of a tiny change in A to the tiny

352
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:43,040
change in x that caused it, is

353
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:45,120
approximately whatever x squared is at

354
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:46,480
that point.

355
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:47,880
And that's an approximation that should

356
00:12:47,880 --> 00:12:49,640
get better and better for smaller and

357
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:52,040
smaller choices of dx.

358
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:53,800
In other words, we don't know what A of

359
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:57,000
x is. That remains a mystery. But, we do

360
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,440
know a property that this mystery

361
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,280
function must have.

362
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:02,280
When you look at two nearby points, for

363
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:05,680
example, 3 and 3.001,

364
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:08,000
consider the change to the output of A

365
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,480
between those two points. The difference

366
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:12,400
between the mystery function evaluated

367
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,200
at 3.001

368
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:16,320
and evaluated at 3.

369
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:18,400
That change divided by the difference in

370
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:20,680
the input values, which in this case is

371
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:22,680
0.001,

372
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:24,760
should be about equal to the value of x

373
00:13:24,760 --> 00:13:27,000
squared for the starting input, in this

374
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,400
case, 3 squared.

375
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:32,360
And this relationship between tiny

376
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:34,400
changes to the mystery function and the

377
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:36,840
values of x squared itself is true at

378
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:39,360
all inputs, not just three.

379
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:40,840
That doesn't immediately tell us how to

380
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,240
find A of X, but it provides a very

381
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:46,280
strong clue that we can work with.

382
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:47,520
And there's nothing special about the

383
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:50,040
graph x squared here. Any function

384
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:52,480
defined as the area under some graph has

385
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:55,880
this property that dA divided by dx, a

386
00:13:55,880 --> 00:13:58,360
slight nudge to the output of A divided

387
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,040
by a slight nudge to the input that

388
00:14:00,040 --> 00:14:01,280
caused it,

389
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:02,880
is about equal to the height of the

390
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:06,080
graph at that point.

391
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:07,800
Again, that's an approximation that gets

392
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:09,920
better and better for smaller choices of

393
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:11,560
dx.

394
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:13,680
And here, we're stumbling into another

395
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:17,040
big idea from calculus, derivatives.

396
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:19,920
This ratio dA divided by dx is called

397
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,760
the derivative of A,

398
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:23,680
or more technically, the derivative is

399
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:26,080
whatever this ratio approaches as dx

400
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:28,120
gets smaller and smaller.

401
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:29,960
I'll dive much more deeply into the idea

402
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:32,120
of a derivative in the next video, but

403
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:34,160
loosely speaking, it's a measure of how

404
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:36,400
sensitive a function is to small changes

405
00:14:36,400 --> 00:14:37,880
in its input.

406
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:39,400
You'll see as the series goes on that

407
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:41,280
there are many, many ways that you can

408
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:43,320
visualize a derivative, depending on

409
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:44,720
what function you're looking at and how

410
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:46,240
you think about tiny nudges to its

411
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:48,839
output.

412
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:50,680
And we care about derivatives because

413
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,880
they help us solve problems. And in our

414
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:55,000
little exploration here, we already have

415
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:56,600
a slight glimpse of one way that they're

416
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:57,760
used.

417
00:14:57,760 --> 00:14:59,560
They are the key to solving integral

418
00:14:59,560 --> 00:15:01,839
questions, problems that require finding

419
00:15:01,839 --> 00:15:04,280
the area under a curve.

420
00:15:04,280 --> 00:15:05,920
Once you gain enough familiarity with

421
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:08,040
computing derivatives, you'll be able to

422
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:10,000
look at a situation like this one where

423
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,800
you don't know what a function is, but

424
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:13,480
you do know that its derivative should

425
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:14,960
be x squared,

426
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,160
and from that, reverse engineer what the

427
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:20,160
function must be.

428
00:15:20,640 --> 00:15:22,800
And this back and forth between

429
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:25,040
and derivatives, where the derivative of

430
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:27,480
a function for the area under a graph

431
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:29,360
gives you back the function defining the

432
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:30,920
graph itself,

433
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:32,760
is called the fundamental theorem of

434
00:15:32,760 --> 00:15:34,160
calculus.

435
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:36,160
It ties together the two big ideas of

436
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:38,240
integrals and derivatives.

437
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:40,480
And it shows how, in some sense, each

438
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:44,320
one is an inverse of the other.

439
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,040
All of this is only a high-level view,

440
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:48,760
just a peek at some of the core ideas

441
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:50,720
that emerge in calculus. And what

442
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:52,480
follows in the series are the details

443
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:55,040
for derivatives and integrals and more.

444
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:56,800
At all points, I want you to feel that

445
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,560
you could have invented calculus

446
00:15:58,560 --> 00:15:59,760
yourself.

447
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:01,200
That if you drew the right pictures and

448
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:02,880
played with each idea in just the right

449
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:03,920
way,

450
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:05,680
these formulas and rules and constructs

451
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:07,440
that are presented could have just as

452
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:09,160
easily popped out naturally from your

453
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:12,280
own explorations.

454
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,240
And before you go, it would feel wrong

455
